<section>
    <h2>The Problem</h2>

    <p>Modern web development has a spacing problem. We've all been there:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Arbitrary pixel values scattered throughout stylesheets</li>
        <li>Inconsistent spacing that "looks right" but has no underlying logic</li>
        <li>Responsive designs that break at unexpected viewport sizes</li>
        <li>Z-index wars with values climbing into the thousands</li>
        <li>Typography that doesn't scale harmoniously with layout</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Design systems attempt to solve this with spacing tokens like <code>$spacing-sm</code>, <code>$spacing-md</code>,
        <code>$spacing-lg</code>. But these are often arbitrary choices — why is "medium" 16px instead of 14px or 18px?
        Why these breakpoints and not others?
    </p>

    <p>unit.gl takes a different approach. Instead of arbitrary tokens, we use <strong>mathematical
            relationships</strong> rooted in typography, music theory, and the physical world.</p>
</section>
